  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how plastic pollution moves through an Arctic marine food web and where exposure has been observed. Arrows link humans, seabirds, seals, whales, fish, zooplankton, benthic invertebrates, and a polar bear, indicating transfer of plastics through feeding relationships across sea ice, open water, and the seafloor.\n\nThe main message is that plastics and especially microplastics are widespread but unevenly documented among species. Surface-feeding seabirds are highlighted as having the highest reported levels of plastic ingestion, while several mid-water-feeding species appear to show low or no detectable ingested microplastics. Land animals, represented by the polar bear, are shown as exposed through ingestion or entanglement. Marine mammals and fish are also connected to lower-trophic prey such as zooplankton and small fish, suggesting plastics can pass upward through the ecosystem.\n\nHumans are included at the top left, with a note that health effects remain poorly understood even though microplastics have been found in organs such as the lungs, intestines, and placenta. The figure also notes an important knowledge gap: many Arctic species have not yet been examined for plastic ingestion, so current understanding is incomplete.",
